---
/**
 * The platforms, with what CI proves about each.
 *
 * The list and the run set come from src/generated/targets.json, which
 * scripts/targets.mjs reads out of the workflow and README. What is new here
 * is src/generated/proofs.json: scripts/proofs.mjs reads the same workflow for
 * the two loops and the job graph, so the page can separate three claims that
 * are usually collapsed into the word "supported" - the library type-checks,
 * the object is audited for what it asks the machine for, and the program is
 * run somewhere that is that platform.
 *
 * A target's row is therefore never a promise: every cell is a job, a step and
 * a line number.
 */
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import Measure from "../components/Measure.astro";
import Lifted from "../components/Lifted.astro";
import platforms from "../generated/targets.json";
import proofs from "../generated/proofs.json";
import { SPELLED } from "../lib/lessons";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

/* The generated shapes, named. A JSON import gives every array the union of
 * its elements' shapes, and a target with no cross-compile line has an empty
 * one; naming the types keeps the template reading like data rather than like
 * a cast. */
interface Build {
  job: string;
  jobName: string;
  step: string | null;
  line: number;
}

interface TargetProof {
  target: string;
  typechecked: boolean;
  audited: boolean;
  ran: boolean;
  how: string | null;
  builds: Build[];
}

interface Runner {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  line: number;
  steps: string[];
}

const ranCount = SPELLED[platforms.ran.length];
const targets = proofs.targets as unknown as TargetProof[];
const runners = proofs.runners as unknown as Runner[];
const audited = targets.filter((entry) => entry.audited);
const typecheck = proofs.loops.typecheck;
const audit = proofs.loops.audit;

/* The audit loop's own spellings that are not one of the nine. Today that is
 * the Windows GNU target, which the type-check loop calls x86_64-w64-mingw32
 * and the audit loop calls x86_64-windows-gnu: one platform, two of the
 * compiler's names for it. scripts/proofs.mjs maps it and then checks its
 * audited set against the one scripts/targets.mjs derives from README, so a
 * real divergence fails the build instead of reaching this paragraph. */
const otherSpelling = audit.values.filter(
  (target) => !platforms.typechecked.includes(target),
);
---

<Page
  kind="TechArticle"
  title="Platforms"
  summary="What CI proves per target: the library type-checks by cross-compiling, an iyi program's object is audited for the symbols it leaves undefined, and the program is linked and run on the platform itself. Three checks, and a target reaches a different number of them."
>
  <section class="wrap head">
    <p class="label">Platforms</p>
    <h1>Compiles for <Measure of="targets" />. Runs on {ranCount}, every build.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Most projects say "supports the platform" and mean the code generator had
      no objection. Here it means three different checks, and a target reaches
      a different number of them. This page is the table, and every cell in it
      is a job and a line in{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/iyilang/iyi/blob/master/.github/workflows/iyi.yml">
        one workflow
      </a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>What each check catches</h2>
    <ol class="senses">
      <li>
        <strong>Type-checked.</strong> The library's platform files for that
        target compile, by cross-compiling with no linking. Catches a
        declaration that went missing from <code>src/lib_c</code> or{" "}
        <code>src/crystal/system</code>, which is invisible to every other job.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Audited.</strong> An iyi program is cross-compiled for the
        target and the object is read for the symbols it leaves undefined.
        Catches a call nothing on that platform supplies, which a type-check
        cannot see.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Run.</strong> The object is linked with the target's own
        toolchain and executed, and what it prints is compared with what the
        same program printed on the machine that built it. The only check that
        can fail for a reason the first two cannot reach.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>The table</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="What CI proves per target">
      <table class="matrix">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">target</th>
            <th scope="col">type-checked</th>
            <th scope="col">object audited</th>
            <th scope="col">run</th>
            <th scope="col">object built by</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {targets.map((entry) => (
            <tr class={entry.ran ? "ran" : undefined}>
              <th scope="row"><code>{entry.target}</code></th>
              <td>{entry.typechecked ? "yes" : "no"}</td>
              <td>{entry.audited ? "yes" : "no"}</td>
              <td>{entry.ran ? entry.how : "no"}</td>
              <td class="where">
                {entry.builds.length > 0
                  ? [...new Set(entry.builds.map((build) => build.jobName))].join(", ")
                  : "built where it is run"}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">
      The {ranCount} that run are {platforms.ran.map((target) => platforms.how[target as keyof typeof platforms.how]).join(", ")}.
      The rest type-check and have their objects audited, which is a weaker
      claim, and this page makes the difference visible rather than averaging
      it into one word.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>The two loops, as they are written</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <h3>{typecheck.step}</h3>
        <p class="sub">
          {typecheck.values.length} targets, in the job{" "}
          <code>{typecheck.job}</code>. Cross-compiling emits an object and
          links nothing, so a library missing from the runner is not a failure
          and a declaration missing from the source is.
        </p>
        <Lifted
          text={typecheck.text}
          caption="the type-check loop"
          cite={typecheck.cite}
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>{audit.step}</h3>
        <p class="sub">
          {audit.values.length} targets crossed with{" "}
          {audit.samples.length} programs ({audit.samples.join(", ")}), each
          object read for what it leaves undefined. The programs are not a
          shorter list for a reason: each carries per-platform branches, and a
          branch only its own platform compiles is a branch nobody checks.
        </p>
        <Lifted
          text={audit.text}
          caption="the audit loop"
          cite={audit.cite}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    {otherSpelling.length > 0 && (
      <p class="sub note">
        The audit loop writes {otherSpelling.join(", ")}, which is the same
        platform the type-check loop lists under another of the compiler's
        names for it. The table above maps the two, and the generator behind it
        checks its audited set against the one read from the repository's own
        prose, so a spelling that turns into a real difference fails the build
        rather than this paragraph.
      </p>
    )}
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Where a cross-compiled object goes to be run</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      One job builds the objects for the other platforms; {runners.length}{" "}
      jobs consume them. They are listed here by their own names, and each
      appears because it says it needs{" "}
      <code>{proofs.producer.id}</code>, not because this page decided it was
      relevant.
    </p>
    <ul class="runners">
      {runners.map((runner) => (
        <li>
          <span class="job">{runner.name}</span>
          <span class="steps">{runner.steps.slice(0, 3).join(" / ")}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="sub">
      Windows is in that list and not in the run set: its objects are built and
      probed for what they ask the operating system for, and no job runs a
      Windows binary and compares what it printed. The{" "}
      <a href={`${base}why/#portability`}>why page</a> says what that means for
      somebody considering it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap next">
    <div class="hero-cta">
      <a class="cta" href={`${base}download/`}>Download a build</a>
      <a class="cta ghost" href={`${base}playground/`}>The wasm32 build, in your browser</a>
      <a class="cta ghost" href={`${base}why/#portability`}>Why this matters</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    section {
      padding-block: clamp(1.2rem, 2.5vw, 2rem);
    }

    .head {
      padding-block-start: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    }

    .head h1 {
      font-size: var(--step-4);
      margin-block: 0.5rem 0.8rem;
    }

    .lede {
      color: var(--graphite);
      max-width: 62ch;
    }

    h2 {
      font-size: var(--step-2);
      margin-block-end: 0.6rem;
    }

    h3 {
      font-size: var(--step-0);
      margin-block-end: 0.4rem;
    }

    .sub {
      color: var(--graphite);
      max-width: 62ch;
      margin-block-end: 1rem;
    }

    .note {
      color: var(--mute);
    }

    .senses {
      margin: 0;
      padding-inline-start: 1.2rem;
      display: grid;
      gap: 0.7rem;
      color: var(--graphite);
      max-width: 68ch;
    }

    .senses strong {
      color: var(--ink);
    }

    .matrix {
      width: 100%;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      font-size: var(--step--1);
      color: var(--graphite);
      margin-block-end: 1rem;
    }

    .matrix th {
      text-align: start;
      font-weight: 500;
    }

    .matrix thead th {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--step--2);
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--mute);
      padding-block-end: 0.4rem;
      border-block-end: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
      white-space: nowrap;
    }

    .matrix td,
    .matrix tbody th {
      padding-block: 0.5rem;
      padding-inline-end: 1rem;
      border-block-end: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
      vertical-align: top;
    }

    /* A run target is the strongest row on the table, so it carries the
     * accent on its own edge. No colour anywhere else: the other rows are not
     * failures. */
    .matrix tbody tr.ran th {
      box-shadow: inset 2px 0 0 var(--brand);
      padding-inline-start: 0.6rem;
    }

    .where {
      color: var(--mute);
    }

    .cols {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr));
      gap: clamp(1.2rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .runners {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: 0.5rem;
      max-width: 60rem;
    }

    .runners li {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 0.2rem 1rem;
      padding-block-end: 0.5rem;
      border-block-end: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
    }

    .job {
      color: var(--ink);
      font-weight: 600;
    }

    .steps {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--step--2);
      color: var(--mute);
      margin-inline-start: auto;
    }

    .next {
      padding-block-end: 3rem;
    }

    .hero-cta {
      display: flex;
      gap: 0.8rem;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      margin-block-end: 1.5rem;
    }
  </style>
</Page>
